---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="LLM vs. Logistic Regression Classifier Baseline · San Lee"
  description="A TF-IDF and logistic-regression baseline scores the same 54 hand-labeled rows as the LLM. The comparison tests whether the added model cost is justified."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/projects/classifier-baseline.html"
  ogType="article"
>

  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* Baseline vs LLM fork (portfolio ADR-004). Page-scoped: why an LLM at
       all is the decision, not the bake-off percentages (the table below
       carries those).

       THE PLATE NOW TAKES THE GALLERY LADDER WIDTH (ADR-014). The old
       `max-width: 260px` rule here held viewBox units at 1:1 with CSS px at
       every width. The MONOGRAPH figure ladder supersedes that rule. The
       markup wraps the plate in `<div class="fig fig--plate"
       data-place="spine">`, and assets/style.css gives it `--fig-plate`
       (24rem). That stylesheet now sets the plate's `width`, `max-width`,
       `height`, `display`, and centring margins, and the rhythm ladder sets
       the air around it. This island re-states none of them.

       A BROKEN 1:1 SCALE OWES A MEASUREMENT. CLAUDE.md states that rule. I
       measured the plate on 2026-08-31 with the pinned Playwright. It renders
       272.0px at a 320px viewport, which is scale 1.046. It renders 312.0px
       at 360, 342.0px at 390, and 382.0px at 430. It caps at 384.0px, scale
       1.477, once the content track reaches 24rem. 320px is therefore the
       worst case for the microtext floor, and .bf-micro below meets the floor
       there. */
    .bake-fork .bf-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .bake-fork .bf-lane {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
    .bake-fork .bf-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    /* The microtext floor (CLAUDE.md, ADR-014 section 5): no SVG text node
       renders under 9px at a 320px viewport. This rule declared 8px. That
       rendered 8.00px on the old 1:1 plate, and 8.37px on the ladder. The
       declared unit steps up to 9px. The plate does not grow under a phone
       breakpoint. A plate that grows only under a phone breakpoint renders
       its text smaller again above that breakpoint, which reverses the floor.
       At a 320px viewport the five .bf-micro nodes now render 9.42px each.
       They still clear their boxes. The widest node is "same rows both arms ·
       paired McNemar", and it measures 194.6 units inside the 260-unit
       viewBox. */
    .bake-fork .bf-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 9px;
    }
    .bake-fork .bf-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* Gold target: ink solid — both arms face the same human set. Dual-
       encoded by word + stroke weight, not colour alone. */
    .bake-fork .bf-gold {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--text);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .bake-fork .bf-gold-text {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    /* The stroke rank inside this plate holds ink > strong > quiet (PL-10).
       .bf-gold keeps --text, the ink rank. .bf-box takes --stroke-figure, the
       strong rank, so the dark retune reaches every box. The edges and their
       arrow markers keep --muted, the quiet rank. In the dark theme the
       figure ground scopes --muted up one rank, so the edges keep their step
       above the lifted ground. */
    .bake-fork marker path { fill: var(--muted); }

    /* CAPTION CSS: THIS PAGE DELETED ITS LOCAL COPY (ADR-014, OC-F closed).
       assets/style.css now holds the two-slot grammar (.fig, .fig figcaption,
       .fig-what, .fig-limit), the global `figcaption code { overflow-wrap:
       anywhere }` rule, and the `figcaption a` rule. The copy that stood here
       came from src/pages/projects/context-architecture.astro at 9d81caa. An
       earlier session copied that block rather than extract it, because a
       concurrent session held the shared stylesheet. That session merged, so
       this lane deletes the copy.

       Two reasons the local copy recorded still bind, so this comment keeps
       them. First, a caption on this page names an artifact file, and a name
       is one unbreakable token. Without the overflow-wrap rule the caption
       widens the page at 320px. Second, lane N measured a link inside a
       figcaption at 1.97:1 in the dark theme, on the browser default.
       assets/style.css now ships both rules for every page, so a caption here
       may hold a link. No caption here holds one, because the paragraph above
       the strip already links the two records it rests on. */

    /* The plate's caption. It holds one slot and not two. A bare plate takes
       a <div class="fig fig--plate"> wrapper and never a <figure> (gate law),
       so the two-slot rule R-C does not reach it. The consolidated .fig-what
       rule gives the note voice, the colour, and the size. Only the margin is
       local: the rhythm ladder reaches direct grid children, and this
       paragraph sits inside the wrapper. */
    .fig--plate > .fig-what { margin: 14px 0 0; }

    /* cb-s1. The two label collapses, re-laid as a strip (ADR-013).

       A STRIP and not a plate. The two rows re-lay text nodes that already sat
       in the card, so the strip cannot break claim parity, and the text stays
       HTML where contrast-check.cjs still reads it. No gate reads SVG text.

       The counts stay in the rows, because a strip does not move its text
       node. That is the one carrier difference from .bake-fork above, whose
       comment records the opposite rule for a plate.

       No hue and no marker. Oxide (--accent-warm) on this page means the
       baseline's wrong answer inside the live demo, and the demo block's own
       comment records that reading. A miss the baseline made in a recorded
       eval is not that state, so every row is a word and a rule, never a
       colour. This block adds no id to the document.

       Rows below 48rem, two columns above it. The house grammar is al-s1 on
       autonomy-ladder.astro: the claim, then the thing it resolves to. The
       strip sits inside a .decision card, whose padding is `22px 0 30px`. The
       figure ladder (ADR-014) adds 14px of inline padding to every figure
       above 640px, and drops it at phone widths (PL-6). So the strip sits 14px
       in on a desktop card, and it runs the full track on a phone. The wide
       promotion never reaches this card: a figure inside .decision keeps the
       entry's own measure (A5). `data-place="spine"` here therefore states
       the per-kind width policy for a strip, and it changes no width today.
       Nothing here is a link, so scripts/hit-target.cjs has no standalone
       anchor to measure. */
    .cb-miss-strip {
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 16px 0 0;
    }
    .cb-miss-strip li {
      min-width: 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
      padding: 11px 0;
    }
    .cb-miss-strip li:first-child {
      border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    }
    .cb-miss-from {
      display: block;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
    }
    .cb-miss-to {
      display: block;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      margin-top: 4px;
    }
    @media (min-width: 48rem) {
      .cb-miss-strip li {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 14rem;
        gap: 4px 24px;
        align-items: start;
      }
      .cb-miss-to {
        margin-top: 0;
      }
    }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav current="work" />

  <style is:inline>
    /* ----- Page-specific styles only: the "run the loser" demo -------------
       Nothing here is reusable yet, so none of it goes in assets/style.css.
       Two constraints shaped it. First, the palette is two hues and the second
       one (--accent-warm) means "the record of something that was taken back"
       -- which is exactly what a baseline's wrong answer is, so the wrong class
       gets the warm token and nothing else invents a colour. Second, no text is
       ever dimmed with opacity (ADR-009): quieter text takes a quieter TOKEN,
       and opacity is reserved for the bar tracks, which carry no text. */

    /* This lane DELETED the demo's own top and bottom margins. It did not
       out-cascade them (ADR-014, PL-2). #baseline-demo is a direct child of
       main.wrap, so the rhythm ladder owns its seam, and every gap on this
       page is exactly one stated value. */

    .bdemo-kicker {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      letter-spacing: 0.1em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--muted);
      margin: 0 0 10px;
    }

    /* Chips. A 44px minimum on both axes is the mobile tap contract, and it is
       set here rather than left to the text's line-height, which would shrink
       with the font. */
    .bdemo-chips {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 8px;
      margin: 0 0 22px;
    }
    .bdemo-chip {
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      color: var(--text);
      background: transparent;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
      padding: 10px 14px;
      min-height: 44px;
      cursor: pointer;
      transition: border-color 0.15s ease, background-color 0.15s ease;
    }
    .bdemo-chip:hover { border-color: var(--muted); }
    .bdemo-chip.is-on {
      border-color: var(--accent);
      background: var(--accent-soft);
    }
    .bdemo-chip:focus-visible {
      outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
      outline-offset: 3px;
    }

    /* The free-text field, ruled rather than boxed, matching .glossary-filter. */
    .bdemo-label {
      display: block;
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      letter-spacing: 0.1em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--muted);
      margin: 0 0 6px;
    }
    .bdemo-input {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      /* border-box or the 1px border and the padding push this past the grid
         track and the page scrolls sideways on a phone. */
      box-sizing: border-box;
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      line-height: 1.5;
      color: var(--text);
      background: transparent;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 0;
      padding: 10px 12px;
      min-height: 106px;
      resize: vertical;
      transition: border-color 0.15s ease;
    }
    .bdemo-input::placeholder { color: var(--muted); }
    .bdemo-input:hover { border-color: var(--muted); }
    .bdemo-input:focus-visible {
      outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
      outline-offset: 3px;
      border-color: var(--accent);
    }

    .bdemo-status {
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
      margin: 10px 0 24px;
      min-height: 1.5em;
    }

    /* Two panels, collapsing to one column with no breakpoint. */
    .bdemo-cols {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(255px, 1fr));
      gap: 28px 34px;
    }
    .bdemo-panel {
      border-top: 2px solid var(--rule-strong);
      padding-top: 14px;
      min-width: 0;
    }
    .bdemo-h {
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-weight: var(--display-weight);
      font-size: 1rem;
      letter-spacing: -0.01em;
      margin: 0 0 16px;
    }

    .bdemo-axis { margin: 0 0 22px; }
    .bdemo-axis-name {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      color: var(--muted);
      margin: 0 0 2px;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    .bdemo-verdict {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 1.15rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      color: var(--text);
      margin: 0 0 10px;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    .bdemo-verdict.is-wrong { color: var(--accent-warm); }
    .bdemo-verdict.is-right { color: var(--accent); }

    /* Bars. The track is the only place opacity appears, and it carries no
       text -- see the block header. */
    .bdemo-bar-row {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 7.6em) 1fr 3.4em;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 8px;
      margin: 0 0 5px;
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.72rem;
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    }
    .bdemo-bar-name {
      color: var(--muted);
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    .bdemo-bar-name.is-top { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 500; }
    .bdemo-bar-name.is-wrong { color: var(--accent-warm); font-weight: 500; }
    .bdemo-bar-track {
      display: block;
      height: 9px;
      min-width: 0;
      background: var(--border);
    }
    .bdemo-bar-fill {
      display: block;
      height: 100%;
      background: var(--rule-strong);
    }
    .bdemo-bar-fill.is-top { background: var(--accent); }
    .bdemo-bar-fill.is-wrong { background: var(--accent-warm); }
    .bdemo-bar-val { color: var(--text-secondary); text-align: right; }

    .bdemo-nosignal {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: var(--accent-warm);
      margin: 0;
    }

    .bdemo-missing,
    .bdemo-ref-note,
    .bdemo-ref-empty {
      font-size: 0.88rem;
      line-height: 1.55;
      font-weight: 500;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
      margin: 0 0 10px;
    }
    .bdemo-ref-prov,
    .bdemo-prov {
      font-size: 0.82rem;
      line-height: 1.55;
      font-weight: 500;
      color: var(--muted);
      margin: 14px 0 0;
    }
    .bdemo-prov { margin-top: 26px; }

    /* The recorded strip. Three columns, no <table>: the site's table rules are
       tuned for the wide comparison tables and would fight a three-cell strip
       inside an already-narrow panel. */
    .bdemo-ref-grid { margin: 0 0 14px; }
    .bdemo-ref-row {
      display: grid;
      /* The key column is the widest of the three because one of its two values
         is the 18-character `operational_domain`; at 1fr it broke mid-word. */
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.55fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 8px;
      padding: 7px 0;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.72rem;
      color: var(--text);
    }
    .bdemo-ref-row.is-head { color: var(--muted); border-bottom-color: var(--rule-strong); }
    .bdemo-ref-k { color: var(--muted); overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .bdemo-ref-row.is-head .bdemo-ref-k { color: var(--muted); }
    .bdemo-ref-v { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .bdemo-ref-row.is-head .bdemo-ref-v { color: var(--muted); }
    .bdemo-ref-v.is-wrong { color: var(--accent-warm); }
  </style>

  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="defense-news-classifier.html">&larr; Back to the classifier</a>
    <h1>Why an LLM at All</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">TF-IDF plus logistic regression. I scored it on the same gold as the LLM.</p>

    <p>
      The classifier writeup measures upgrades on top of a language model: a
      sharper prompt, retrieval, a bigger model for the hard rows. It does not
      answer whether the task needs an LLM.
    </p>
    <p>
      So I built the boring baseline. It puts TF-IDF features into logistic
      regression, one classifier per axis. I trained it on 300 real snippets
      that the Opus judge graded, which is the scaled set and not v1&rsquo;s
      synthetic one. I scored it once against the same 54 hand-labeled rows.
      Standard, not tuned.
    </p>

    <!-- Decision fork: same text, two arms, one human gold set. Percentages
         stay in the table below (ADR-004 Decision 5 floor — the figure carries
         topology, prose keeps the numbers). No oxide: this is a bake-off, not
         a reversal claim. ADR-010 N/A — no interactive nodes.

         PLATE 01 (ADR-014). The bare plate takes a <div class="fig
         fig--plate"> wrapper and never a <figure>. A <figure> with no
         data-fig fails rule R-N. A <figure> with data-fig needs two caption
         slots, and this caption holds one. data-place="spine" states the
         per-kind width policy for a plate. -->
    <div class="fig fig--plate" data-place="spine">
      <span class="fig-label">Plate 01</span>
      <svg class="bake-fork" viewBox="0 0 260 148" role="img"
           aria-label="Decision fork for the classifier bake-off. One defense-news snippet goes to a classical TF-IDF logistic-regression baseline and to the LLM. Both face the same 54-row human gold set.">
        <title>Decision fork for the classifier bake-off. One defense-news snippet goes to a classical TF-IDF logistic-regression baseline and to the LLM. Both face the same 54-row human gold set.</title>
        <defs>
          <marker id="bf-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                  markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <!-- Shared input on the left. -->
        <text class="bf-lane" x="4" y="12">INPUT</text>
        <rect class="bf-box" x="4" y="48" width="70" height="40" rx="2" />
        <text class="bf-title" x="39" y="68" text-anchor="middle">snippet</text>
        <text class="bf-micro" x="39" y="80" text-anchor="middle">one row</text>

        <!-- Split: classical above, LLM below — both face the same gold. -->
        <path class="bf-edge" d="M 74 68 L 90 68 L 90 40 L 106 40"
              marker-end="url(#bf-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
        <path class="bf-edge" d="M 90 68 L 90 96 L 106 96"
              marker-end="url(#bf-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />

        <text class="bf-lane" x="110" y="12">ARMS</text>
        <rect class="bf-box" x="110" y="22" width="78" height="36" rx="2" />
        <text class="bf-title" x="149" y="38" text-anchor="middle">TF-IDF + LR</text>
        <text class="bf-micro" x="149" y="50" text-anchor="middle">classical</text>

        <rect class="bf-box" x="110" y="78" width="78" height="36" rx="2" />
        <text class="bf-title" x="149" y="94" text-anchor="middle">LLM</text>
        <text class="bf-micro" x="149" y="106" text-anchor="middle">forced tool</text>

        <!-- Merge into shared gold. -->
        <path class="bf-edge" d="M 188 40 L 204 40 L 204 68 L 218 68"
              marker-end="url(#bf-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
        <path class="bf-edge" d="M 188 96 L 204 96 L 204 68" aria-hidden="true" />

        <text class="bf-lane" x="200" y="12">GOLD</text>
        <rect class="bf-gold" x="222" y="48" width="34" height="40" rx="2" />
        <text class="bf-gold-text" x="239" y="68" text-anchor="middle">n=54</text>
        <text class="bf-micro" x="239" y="80" text-anchor="middle">human</text>

        <text class="bf-micro" x="130" y="140" text-anchor="middle">same rows both arms &middot; paired McNemar</text>
      </svg>
      <p class="fig-what">
        One snippet, two arms, one human gold set. The table below is the score;
        the fork is why the comparison is fair.
      </p>
    </div>

    <table class="metrics" id="bakeoff">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Against human gold, n=54</th><th>Classical baseline</th><th>The LLM</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Category accuracy</td><td>72.2%</td><td>92.6%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Domain accuracy</td><td>66.7%</td><td>92.6%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cost per article</td><td>$0.00</td><td>one API call</td></tr>
        <tr><td>All 54 rows, wall-clock</td><td>4.8&nbsp;ms</td><td>network-bound, seconds</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      The LLM wins by 20 points on category and 26 on domain. Both arms ran on
      identical rows, so a test can measure the gap. A paired exact McNemar
      returns <strong>p=0.013</strong> on category and <strong>p=0.0005</strong>
      on domain. I also counted the disagreements. The baseline alone was wrong
      14 times to the LLM&rsquo;s 3 on category, and 15 to 1 on domain
      (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/archive/017-classical-baseline-bakeoff.md">ADR-017</a>,
      full report in
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/evals/baseline_eval.txt">baseline_eval.txt</a>).
    </p>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Where the baseline broke</p>
      <p>The misses do not spread evenly. The baseline collapses toward
        whichever class is lexically loud.</p>
      <!-- cb-s1. Two collapses, one shape. The strip re-lays the two clauses
           this card already carried, and the counts travel inside them: a
           strip does not move its text node, so the figures stay where the
           reader reads them. That is the carrier difference from .bake-fork
           above, whose comment records the opposite rule for a plate. The two
           sentences under the strip are the argument, and they stay as
           prose.

           STRIP 02 (ADR-014). This is the second figure in reading order,
           and a strip takes the spine. -->
      <figure class="fig cb-s1" data-fig="strip" data-place="spine">
        <span class="fig-label">Strip 02</span>
        <ul class="cb-miss-strip" aria-label="Two labels the baseline collapsed, and the class each one collapsed into">
          <li>
            <span class="cb-miss-from">five of six <code>policy</code> stories</span>
            <span class="cb-miss-to">came back as <code>operations</code></span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="cb-miss-from">six of eleven <code>land</code> rows</span>
            <span class="cb-miss-to">came back as <code>air</code></span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <figcaption>
          <span class="fig-what">Both collapses run in one direction. The
          baseline assigns the lexically louder class, and the quieter class
          loses its rows to it. ADR-017 and
          <code>baseline_eval.txt</code> record the bake-off, and the paragraph
          above links both.</span>
          <span class="fig-limit">The strip shows two boundaries, not the whole
          confusion matrix. It does not show why these two boundaries are hard.
          The two sentences below carry that reason.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>Those are the labels that depend on what <em>kind of claim</em> an
        article makes, not which words it contains. A treaty and a deployment
        share a vocabulary; only one of them is policy.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>What that means for the spend:</strong> the
        LLM buys the boundaries that need reading comprehension.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Two handicaps</p>
      <p>The training labels came from the Opus judge, not from people. So the
        baseline learned to imitate a grader that itself disagrees with humans
        about 5&ndash;6% of the time. Then I tested it against those humans.
        Separately, <code>industry</code> had exactly <strong>one</strong>
        training row, which makes that label structurally unlearnable. The
        baseline scored 0.000 on it, as it had to.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why they stay:</strong> both cut against the
        baseline. The same class of unfairness inflated the BM25 lift until I
        rebuilt the baseline. A margin this size survives the caveats.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="try-it">Run the loser</h2>
    <p>
      This is the same fit, and it runs in your tab. I exported the model as
      data, about 208&nbsp;KB over the wire, with no server and no key. It is
      the arm that <a href="#bakeoff">lost the bake-off</a>. Here you watch the
      wrong class light up. That is faster than a read of the confusion matrix.
    </p>

    <div class="wide bdemo" id="baseline-demo">
      <p class="bdemo-kicker">A row from the gold set, or your own sentence</p>
      <div class="bdemo-chips" id="bdemo-chips" role="group" aria-label="Example rows from the human gold set"></div>

